  <!-- ════ 00 — EXECUTIVE SUMMARY ════ -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="summary">
    <div class="section-label">00</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Executive Summary</h2>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout__label">In one paragraph</div>
      <p>Destination weddings now make up <strong>18–32% of all U.S. weddings</strong> and a $16B+ global market, but the major wedding website platforms (Zola, The Knot, Joy, Minted) were designed for hometown weddings. They handle <em>storytelling and RSVPs</em> well — and stop short of the things that actually break for traveling couples: <strong>group travel coordination, hotel block booking, multi-day itineraries, multilingual communication, real-time updates during the travel window, and group payment collection</strong>. The clearest opportunity is not a prettier wedding website — it's a website-plus-coordination-layer purpose-built for the operational reality of getting 60+ people across borders to one resort.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>The chapters that follow lay out the evidence and the analysis. The path is: <strong>market size and behavior</strong> → <strong>why destination weddings demand different software</strong> → <strong>who the customers are and what they say</strong> → <strong>where today's competitors actually compete</strong> → <strong>the jobs to be done with opportunity scores</strong> → <strong>opportunities, challenges, and a product-market-fit hypothesis</strong>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════ 01 — MARKET CONTEXT ════ -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="market">
    <div class="section-label">01</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Market context &amp; behavioral stats</h2>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout__label">TL;DR</div>
      <p>Destination weddings are a structurally growing, premium, millennial-and-Gen-Z-led segment with predictable economics — a smaller, wealthier guest list traveling further and spending more per attendee than a hometown wedding.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3>The numbers worth memorizing</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="tool-figures">
      <div class="tool-figure-cell">
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__num">18–32%</div>
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__label">of all U.S. weddings are destination weddings (range across The Knot, Destify, FMI)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-figure-cell">
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__num">$16.2B</div>
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__label">Global destination wedding market in 2026, projected $26.8B by 2036 (FMI, 5.1% CAGR)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-figure-cell">
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__num">69%</div>
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__label">of millennials prefer a destination wedding; 49% of Gen Z considering one</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-figure-cell">
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__num">74%</div>
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__label">of engaged couples are already creating a wedding website (Joy)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-figure-cell">
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__num">60–70</div>
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__label">Average destination wedding guest count — vs. 116–141 for traditional</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-figure-cell">
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__num">60–70%</div>
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__label">Average destination wedding RSVP acceptance rate — vs. 80–85% for hometown</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-figure-cell">
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__num">$1,000–$2,000</div>
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__label">Average per-guest spend (flights + accommodation, 4–5 nights)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-figure-cell">
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__num">36%</div>
        <div class="tool-figure-cell__label">of destination couples hire a wedding planner (vs. 24% hometown) — they buy help</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Geographic distribution</h3>
      <p>Most U.S. destination weddings stay domestic. <strong>About 70% are within the U.S.</strong> — Hawaii, Florida, Colorado top the list. Internationally, <strong>Mexico captures 34%</strong> of bookings, Europe 26%, Caribbean 21%. The Dominican Republic and Costa Rica are gaining share as couples seek strong amenities at lower prices than Europe. The market splits cleanly into "fly to a domestic resort or city" and "fly internationally," with very different software needs (passport/visa, multilingual, currency, time zone) between the two.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary class="faq-item__q">Deep dive — Why this market is structurally growing, not a fad</summary>
        <div class="faq-item__a">
          <p><strong>1. Demographic preference.</strong> Millennials are the dominant marrying cohort, and 69% prefer destination weddings. Gen Z is following the same curve. This is a generational shift, not a trend.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Smaller guest lists are a feature, not a bug.</strong> Modern couples increasingly want experience-driven, curated celebrations over large traditional receptions. A destination is both an aesthetic choice and a built-in guest-list filter.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Travel infrastructure normalization.</strong> Group travel, all-inclusive resort packages, and dedicated DW specialists (Destify, Destination Weddings .com, Just Book It) have built mature supply.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Second-marriages contribute disproportionately.</strong> 33% of destination weddings are second marriages — an older, higher-income, more travel-comfortable segment.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Average household income of destination couples is ~$110K</strong> — willing and able to pay for tools that reduce stress.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════ 02 — WHY DESTINATION WEDDINGS BREAK NORMAL SITES ════ -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="why-different">
    <div class="section-label">02</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Why destination weddings break a normal wedding website</h2>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout__label">TL;DR</div>
      <p>A hometown wedding website's job is to share <em>information</em>. A destination wedding website's job is to coordinate <em>logistics, money, and uncertainty</em> across dozens of people, weeks of travel, and (often) multiple languages. The two are different software products.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>Every wedding website needs three core things: a story page, RSVP collection, and a registry. Destination weddings layer on operational requirements that hometown weddings rarely surface.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tool-table-wrap">
      <table class="tool-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th style="width: 26%">Dimension</th><th>Hometown wedding</th><th>Destination wedding</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>RSVP horizon</td><td>3–4 weeks before</td><td>3–5 months before, so guests can book flights/PTO</td></tr>
          <tr><td>RSVP signal</td><td>Yes/No is enough</td><td>"Yes" is provisional until they've booked a room. Couples need a <em>hard</em> RSVP tied to a booking.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Guest info needed</td><td>Meal choice, plus-one</td><td>Arrival date, departure date, passport country, dietary, attendance per side-event, shuttle slot</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Communication cadence</td><td>Save-the-date, invite, day-of</td><td>Continuous from save-the-date through the actual travel window — including last-minute weather/flight updates</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Site shape</td><td>One day, one venue</td><td>Multi-day itinerary: welcome party, rehearsal, ceremony, excursions, farewell brunch</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Accommodation</td><td>Optional hotel block</td><td>Critical — room block code, booking deadline, alternatives, transfer logistics, day-pass rules</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Payment flows</td><td>Registry only</td><td>Group payments for excursions, shuttle, welcome party often required — wedding websites don't handle this</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Language</td><td>One</td><td>Often two or three (couple's, partner's, and host country's)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mobile use</td><td>Pre-event lookup</td><td>Live reference during travel — "what time is the shuttle to the rehearsal dinner"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <blockquote>
        "Our guests need a clear, simple path to booking their travel. A personalized wedding website with room options, rates, flight information, and easy RSVP eliminates the confusion and makes attending feel manageable rather than complicated."
        <cite>Bliss Honeymoons, destination wedding planner</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>The most concrete way to see the gap: most wedding website builders let a couple list a "Travel" page with hotel info as <em>static text</em>. They do not let the couple <strong>see which guests have actually booked a room, push an update to only guests who haven't, or collect $85/person for the welcome-night dinner.</strong> The work then falls back to spreadsheets, group texts, and a travel agent acting as the human integration layer.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════ 03 — CUSTOMER RESEARCH ════ -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="customer">
    <div class="section-label">03</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Customer research</h2>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout__label">TL;DR</div>
      <p>There are <strong>three customers</strong> in this market, not one — the couple, the guest, and the wedding planner / travel agent. Each has a sharply different definition of "the wedding website works." Most platforms optimize only for the couple.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3>The three customers</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="tool-entries dw-personas">
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Buyer · Primary user</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">The Couple</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">
          Age 28–35, household income ~$110K, planning 9–18 months out. Picks the platform.
          <strong>Top fear</strong>
          Looking disorganized to family, getting endless texts asking "wait, what airport?"
          <strong>Success metric</strong>
          Headcount confidence by the 90-day mark + zero individual "did you book yet?" texts.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Heaviest user · Lowest tolerance</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">The Guest</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">
          Spending $1,000–$2,000+ of their own money. Mostly mobile. Will check the site many times before and during travel.
          <strong>Top fear</strong>
          Showing up wrong — wrong outfit, wrong dock, missing the shuttle.
          <strong>Success metric</strong>
          Finding answers without texting the couple. Confidence that they've booked the right thing.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Planner or travel agent</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">The Coordinator</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">
          Present for 36% of destination weddings. Often the de-facto integration layer between the wedding website, the resort, and the guest list.
          <strong>Top fear</strong>
          Late RSVPs causing last-minute resort re-quotes, lost commissions on guests who book outside the block.
          <strong>Success metric</strong>
          Real-time view of who has booked, who has paid, who needs a nudge.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3>What couples say (themes from forums, planner blogs, and platform reviews)</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>"The RSVP isn't real until they've booked."</strong> Couples consistently distinguish between a soft "Yes I'd love to come" and a hard "I have a room." Today these two signals live in different systems.</li>
        <li><strong>"I became a travel agent for my family."</strong> Without a planner, couples answer the same five questions — flight options, airport transfer, passport, dress code for the welcome party, room block deadline — dozens of times.</li>
        <li><strong>"I needed to text 12 people and nobody else."</strong> The ability to message <em>only</em> guests who haven't booked, or only guests arriving Friday, is consistently cited as missing from the major platforms.</li>
        <li><strong>"Last-minute changes are inevitable."</strong> Weather, flight delays, shuttle shifts. Couples want push-notification-style updates, not "hope they check the site again."</li>
        <li><strong>"My partner's family doesn't read English."</strong> Bilingual and multilingual support is requested constantly, especially for U.S./Mexico, U.S./India, and intra-European couples.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What guests complain about (themes from r/weddingshaming, r/wedding, advice columns)</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Surprise costs.</strong> Welcome party fees, day-pass costs, mandatory minimum-stay rules, higher room rates booked through the couple's agent than available on Booking.com.</li>
        <li><strong>Opacity.</strong> Why is the rate $400/night when I can see it cheaper? Is the welcome dinner included? Do I have to attend the rehearsal? Couples are often unclear because <em>the website doesn't have a place to be clear about it</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Difficulty coordinating with other guests.</strong> "Who else is on my flight?" "Is anyone splitting an Airbnb?" There is no group-chat-like layer.</li>
        <li><strong>Pressure to commit early without information.</strong> Many guests are asked to book a $2,000 trip with only a hotel link and an emailed PDF.</li>
      </ul>

      <blockquote>
        "She hyped it up as a tropical vacation with my best people. I took unpaid leave for this trip thinking I'd get some rest."
        <cite>Reddit guest, after arriving to find a community-service retreat surprise</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="note-line">The lesson behind that quote isn't the bait-and-switch — it's that <em>the wedding website was the primary expectation-setting tool, and it failed</em>. Guests use the site to model what they're signing up for. When the model is wrong, the relationship pays for it.</p>

      <h3>What the coordinator/planner says</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Spreadsheets are the de-facto system of record.</strong> Almost every operator interviewed by SquadTrip describes paying-guests-tracking and room-block status as living in a Google Sheet stitched to a Stripe link.</li>
        <li><strong>The wedding website and the travel agent's booking engine are different systems.</strong> Guests are routinely confused about which to use.</li>
        <li><strong>"Chasing payments" is the single most expensive ongoing task.</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════ 04 — COMPETITIVE LANDSCAPE ════ -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="competitive">
    <div class="section-label">04</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Competitive landscape</h2>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout__label">TL;DR</div>
      <p>The category is concentrated at the top (The Knot Worldwide, Zola, Joy), differentiated in the middle by design (Minted, Riley &amp; Grey, Appy Couple), and being attacked from the side by <strong>travel-coordination tools</strong> (SquadTrip, Destify, Destination Weddings .com) that treat the website as a feature of a larger logistics product. No single player owns "destination weddings."</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3>The strategic map</h3>

      <p>Today's players cluster into four groups based on what they actually solve:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>All-in-one wedding platforms</strong> — Zola, The Knot, Joy, WeddingWire. Free websites monetized via registry, vendor marketplace, or ads. Designed for the median U.S. wedding.</li>
        <li><strong>Design-led builders</strong> — Minted, Riley &amp; Grey, Appy Couple, Paperless Post. Sell aesthetic, often paired with matched paper goods.</li>
        <li><strong>Generic site builders adapted</strong> — Squarespace, Wix. Maximum design control but require manual stitching for RSVPs and registry.</li>
        <li><strong>Travel-coordination tools</strong> — SquadTrip, Destify, Destination Weddings .com, Just Book It, Journeys. Treat the wedding website as one surface inside a group-travel-and-payments product. <em>This is the most credible threat to the incumbents in the destination niche.</em></li>
      </ol>

