            <td><span class="tool-dot tool-dot--full" role="img" aria-label="Strong"></span></td>
            <td><span class="tool-dot tool-dot--none" role="img" aria-label="Missing"></span></td>
            <td><span class="tool-dot tool-dot--half" role="img" aria-label="Partial"></span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="note-line">Read the columns: no single platform fills every row. Joy is the strongest pure wedding-website builder for destination needs; SquadTrip is the strongest coordination tool; Destination Weddings .com is the strongest <em>integrated</em> offering but is essentially an agency selling free software to its travel customers. The visible whitespace: a product that combines Joy's design + multilingual depth with SquadTrip's group-payment and room-block coordination.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary class="faq-item__q">Deep dive — Business model snapshots</summary>
        <div class="faq-item__a">
          <p><strong>Zola</strong> — Free websites monetized through registry (cash funds, gift commissions) and stationery. ~225 employees, ~$44M revenue. Strength: registry depth + design library (1,000+ templates). Weakness: U.S.-only registry, no multilingual, no group travel.</p>
          <p><strong>The Knot Worldwide</strong> — Free websites monetized through vendor marketplace (paid lead-gen to local vendors). Global reach via Bodas.net, Matrimonio.com, Hitched. Strength: brand familiarity across generations, vast vendor network. Weakness: legacy product, not destination-optimized.</p>
          <p><strong>Joy</strong> — Genuinely free websites monetized through registry partnerships. Strength: best-in-class UX, free multilingual, free mobile app, customizable RSVPs. Weakness: smaller template library (~60), still no group payments or room-block integration.</p>
          <p><strong>Minted / Riley &amp; Grey</strong> — Premium paper-goods companies that include a website as part of a paid stationery purchase. Strength: aesthetic ceiling. Weakness: minimal logistics tooling.</p>
          <p><strong>Appy Couple</strong> — Paid (~$30–60) wedding website + native mobile app, marketed to "tech-savvy" couples. Strength: push notifications, app-first experience. Weakness: low brand awareness, setup complexity, no payments.</p>
          <p><strong>WedSites</strong> — Newer entrant ($99–199 one-time) emphasizing planning dashboard + true multilingual support. Strength: best multilingual implementation, design control. Weakness: no group payments, smaller distribution.</p>
          <p><strong>SquadTrip</strong> — Group-trip operations platform ($X/transaction). Sells to <em>planners</em>, not couples directly. Strength: payments, BNPL, installment plans, deposit collection. Weakness: not a beautiful wedding website; positioned as a complement.</p>
          <p><strong>Destification players (Destify, Destination Weddings .com, Just Book It, Journeys)</strong> — Travel agencies that give the couple a free website as part of an end-to-end planning service. Revenue from resort commissions. Strength: integrated room blocks + RSVPs. Weakness: customers must use their travel-agent network, limited design flexibility.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════ 05 — JOBS TO BE DONE + OPPORTUNITY SCORES ════ -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="jtbd">
    <div class="section-label">05</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Jobs to be done &amp; opportunity scores</h2>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout__label">TL;DR</div>
      <p>Using Ulwick's <strong>Opportunity = Importance + max(Importance − Satisfaction, 0)</strong> on a 1–10 scale, the highest-opportunity jobs are <strong>"convert intent into a booked room,"</strong> <strong>"collect group payments without spreadsheets,"</strong> <strong>"push real-time updates during the travel window,"</strong> and <strong>"serve the site in the guest's native language."</strong> Storytelling, registry, and aesthetic are all <em>saturated</em> jobs — high satisfaction, low opportunity.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3>How to read the table</h3>
      <p>Importance is how critical the job is to the couple's success (1–10). Satisfaction is how well current platforms do it (1–10). Opportunity score = Importance + max(Importance − Satisfaction, 0); a score above ~12 typically signals a real opening; above 15 is rare and significant.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tool-table-wrap">
      <table class="tool-table tool-table--num dw-jtbd">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th style="width: 40%">Job statement (from the couple's POV)</th>
            <th>Imp.</th>
            <th>Sat.</th>
            <th>Opp.</th>
            <th></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>When I send a save-the-date, help me convert "I plan to come" into a confirmed booked room before my resort deadline.</td>
            <td>10</td><td>3</td>
