    <div class="prose">
      <p class="note-line">Method note: scores are directional, not survey-derived. They reflect synthesis of platform reviews, planner blog content, Reddit/forum patterns, and the comparison gaps identified in Chapter 04. A formal ODI study with 50+ destination-wedding couples would tighten the numbers, but the rank ordering is robust.</p>

      <h3>The pattern</h3>
      <p>The <strong>top four jobs by opportunity score are all logistics, money, communication, and language</strong> — not design or storytelling. The bottom four are all jobs the major platforms already do well. <em>This is the definition of a competitive moat that doesn't compound</em>: incumbents are competing harder and harder on saturated jobs while leaving the underserved jobs to spreadsheets and travel agents.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════ 06 — OPPORTUNITIES, CHALLENGES, PMF ════ -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="pmf">
    <div class="section-label">06</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Opportunities, challenges &amp; PMF hypothesis</h2>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout__label">TL;DR</div>
      <p>The PMF hypothesis: a destination-wedding-native platform that fuses a beautiful, multilingual wedding website with a real coordination layer (room-block booking inside the RSVP, group payments, segmented messaging, push updates) can win — but only if it solves the planner/travel-agent distribution problem and the chicken-and-egg between resort partnerships and couples.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Opportunities (where to invest)</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="tool-entries">
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Opportunity 01</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Hard RSVPs tied to booked rooms</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">Make the "Yes" button on the RSVP form trigger an actual room reservation. Show couples a single dashboard with two columns: said-yes and actually-booked. This requires resort partnerships but is the single highest-leverage feature in this market.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Opportunity 02</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Group payments as a first-class object</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">Couples already collect money for excursions, welcome parties, shuttles. Today it lives in Venmo and Google Sheets. Building this natively (with installments and BNPL, à la SquadTrip) into a wedding website is uncontested.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Opportunity 03</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">True multilingual, not "Google Translate the page"</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">Multicultural couples are growing. WedSites and Joy do this well; Zola and The Knot don't do it at all. A site where each guest is tagged with a language and gets a fully native experience — including RSVP forms and email blasts — is table stakes for the global segment.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Opportunity 04</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Travel-window push updates</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">The 72 hours before and during the wedding generate every emergency. A native mobile app with push notifications, segmented by guest tag (flight cancelled? message only Newark arrivals), turns the wedding website from static to operational.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Opportunity 05</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">The planner / travel-agent channel</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">36% of destination couples hire a planner. Most planners use spreadsheets. A product that's <em>built for the planner</em> — with the couple's website as one surface — turns the planner into a distribution channel. SquadTrip is doing exactly this; an integrated competitor could win on UX.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Opportunity 06</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Guest-to-guest coordination layer</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">"Who else is on my flight" and "anyone splitting an Airbnb" is a real, unsolved guest need. A lightweight directory or opt-in chat feature, owned by the couple but used by guests, has no current competitor.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Challenges (what makes this hard)</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="tool-entries">
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Challenge 01</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Resort &amp; OTA relationships</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">The "hard RSVP tied to a room" feature requires integrations with Booking.com, Expedia Group, or direct contracts with all-inclusive chains. These are slow, regulated, and commission-protected — exactly why agency-led products like Destification have a moat.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Challenge 02</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Free-tier expectation</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">Zola, The Knot, and Joy have trained couples that the website is free. Charging for a destination-specific tool means either bundling with paid stationery (Minted) or capturing money from group-payment transactions (SquadTrip). Pure SaaS pricing to couples will likely fail.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Challenge 03</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">One-time, low-LTV customers</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">A couple buys a wedding website exactly once. CAC has to be low or paid by someone else (a vendor, a registry, a resort). This is why incumbents monetize via the registry tail or vendor marketplace, not the website itself.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Challenge 04</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Cold-start brand trust</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">Guests need to trust the link the couple sends them. Unknown brands trigger friction. The Knot and Zola benefit from name recognition; a new platform has to earn it, often through aesthetic and design-press exposure (Riley &amp; Grey's path).</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Challenge 05</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Wedding planners are conservative buyers</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">Planners have working systems (spreadsheets + Trello + Stripe + email) and switching costs are high. New tools must be dramatically better than the patchwork, not 1.2x better.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Challenge 06</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Seasonality &amp; cohort attrition</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">Demand peaks Q1 (when couples are newly engaged from holiday proposals) and bookings deplete the resort blocks. The product has to feel "alive" 12–18 months before a wedding, which is unusual for a SaaS engagement curve.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Product-market-fit hypothesis</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="tool-entries">
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Bet 01 · Most defensible</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Planner-led platform that exposes a beautiful couple-facing site</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">Sell to wedding planners and travel agents as their operations stack (think: SquadTrip + a beautiful website builder + multilingual). The couple-facing site is a deliverable. Monetize through transaction fees on group payments and resort commissions. <strong>This is the most defensible because it stacks two revenue streams and uses a B2B channel that values quality.</strong></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Bet 02 · Highest velocity</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Couple-direct freemium with paid coordination upgrade</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">Free beautiful wedding website (Joy-quality). $99–199 unlock for "Destination Mode": room-block tracking, group payments, multilingual, push notifications, segmented messaging. Monetize the destination-specific minority of couples (~25% of weddings) at the moment they need real tools. <strong>Faster to launch, but harder to defend against an incumbent that copies the upgrade.</strong></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-entry">
        <div class="tool-entry__overline">Bet 03 · Wedge play</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__title">Group-travel-payments add-on, distributed via incumbent integrations</div>
        <div class="tool-entry__body">Build the group-payments + segmented-messaging layer as a Zapier-style add-on that plugs into Zola, The Knot, and Joy. Charge couples or planners per transaction. <strong>Lowest moat, but lowest cost to validate demand</strong> — and a natural acquisition target for an incumbent that decides to enter destination.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════ 07 — SYNTHESIS ════ -->
  <section class="tool-section" id="synthesis">
    <div class="section-label">07</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Synthesis</h2>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout__label">The one-line takeaway</div>
      <p>Wedding websites for destination weddings is a category in which the work has outgrown the product. The next winner won't make a prettier site — it will fold the spreadsheet, the group chat, the room block, and the payment link into the site itself.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>If you remember nothing else from this overview, remember the three asymmetries:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Demand is concentrated in a small, high-spending, growing segment</strong> — 18–32% of all U.S. weddings, $1,000–$2,000 per guest, growing 5–7% annually, dominated by millennials and Gen Z.</li>
        <li><strong>Software supply is concentrated on the wrong jobs</strong> — every major incumbent competes on design, registry, and storytelling (all saturated), while logistics, payments, communication, and language remain underserved.</li>
        <li><strong>The integration layer between "wedding website" and "group travel" is unowned</strong> — the most credible new entrants (SquadTrip, agency-led platforms) approach it from the travel side; the most established players (Zola, The Knot, Joy) approach it from the website side. Neither has crossed the gap.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The product-market-fit question is therefore less "is there demand?" — there obviously is — and more "who reaches the couple at the moment they realize their hometown wedding website won't carry the weight of a destination wedding?" Whoever wins that moment, with the operational depth to deliver, owns the category.</p>

      <blockquote>
        The wedding is the moment. The website is the system of record for everything around it. For destination weddings, the system of record needs to behave like operational software, not a digital scrapbook.
        <cite>Synthesis</cite>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="note-line dw-end">— end of overview —</p>

